WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:06.269
In this video, we'll go back over everything we learned in this course

2
00:00:06.269 --> 00:00:09.410
and give you some jumping off points for further exploration.

3
00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:12.000
So, what have we covered today?

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.960
We've translated Unity concepts and terms into their Unreal Engine counterparts.

5
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.960
We exported our assets from Unity as FBXs and imported them into Unreal.

6
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.699
We recreated logic contains within scripts and animation controllers that couldn't make the jump.

7
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.700
We then also rebuilt materials and player interactions that didn't come through.

8
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:40.140
Next, we were able to hone our look and feel through the use of post-processing and lighting.

9
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.939
And we've learned about some of Unreal Engine's unique features like level blueprints,

10
00:00:44.939 --> 00:00:50.340
material parameter collections, and some of the features of Unreal's modeling mode.

11
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:56.420
Finally, we packaged our project into an executable file that we can share with the world.

12
00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.159
For next steps, there's a number of things you can do to take your project further.

13
00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:04.260
For example, you could try using the enhanced input system for player input,

14
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.879
which allows for several different keys across gamepads, keyboards, et cetera,

15
00:01:08.879 --> 00:01:12.519
to all create the same interactive sequences in Unreal.

16
00:01:12.519 --> 00:01:16.959
Also, you can try using the sequencer in Unreal Engine to drive other kinds of animation,

17
00:01:16.959 --> 00:01:19.060
environmental changes, et cetera.

18
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:23.260
This can be done with cameras and animating assets to a greater degree

19
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.459
than what we brought over from Unity.

20
00:01:25.459 --> 00:01:29.599
You can also try additional world-building tools using the landscape tools,

21
00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:32.000
as well as things like foliage, trees.

22
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.519
Unreal has a very robust system for painting and creating these kinds of environments.

23
00:01:36.519 --> 00:01:41.799
You can also try creating or modifying the visual effects in the project using Niagara,

24
00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:45.400
which is the more advanced particle system in Unreal Engine,

25
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.319
if you want to go beyond what you might have done with, say, the VFX graph in Unity.

26
00:01:50.319 --> 00:01:54.799
You can also try adding a UI, which might include elements like a quit button,

27
00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:58.540
using the UMG system in Unreal, which is how we create widgets

28
00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.260
and other kinds of HUDs and overlays.

29
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.400
You can also try going into FAB and importing Unity assets from there

30
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.000
directly into Unreal to understand how that pipeline might work.

31
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.740
Additionally, there is the USD file format,

32
00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:16.860
which stands for Universal Scene Description, developed by friends at Pixar,

33
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.560
and that is becoming a more and more common industry standard

34
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.699
for bringing 3D data from one DCC application to another,

35
00:02:24.699 --> 00:02:27.539
both Unreal Engine and Unity support USD

36
00:02:27.539 --> 00:02:30.500
with the appropriate plugins and packages enabled.

37
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.099
And so that might be another way to move between these programs,

38
00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:36.300
especially if you want to go back and forth,

39
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.240
because it can actually be nondestructive,

40
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.439
and you can modify the USD file without needing to export it

41
00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:46.500
or change things as you go between your DCC applications.

42
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.439
And then, of course, the FAB marketplace is full of a very large number of tools

43
00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:54.400
which can benefit both the Unity and Unreal Engine pipelines.

44
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.439
So anything that you might want to do across either of those applications,

45
00:02:58.439 --> 00:03:01.500
there is a good chance that FAB has something you need,

46
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.639
whether it be on the coding and interactive side

47
00:03:04.639 --> 00:03:06.639
or just general assets.

48
00:03:06.639 --> 00:03:08.000
Thank you for joining us today.

49
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.099
We hope you found this helpful and learned a lot,

50
00:03:10.099 --> 00:03:12.800
and we look forward to seeing you in a future course.

51
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.240
And Whit and I both wish you luck on your Unreal Engine journey.
